      "why": "Readers should note that these fields are all in a single temperate region on similar soils, so extrapolation to other climates or to horticultural systems is not supported by this dataset."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c13",
      "page": 5,
      "why": "Organic management was associated with higher soil invertebrate species richness in this paired comparison. Effects on microbial biomass and earthworm abundance were not detectable at the sample sizes available."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c2",
      "page": 1,
      "why": "We compared soil biological indicators across paired organic and conventional fields over three growing seasons. Organic management was associated with higher invertebrate species richness, while effects on microbial biomass and on soil fauna abundance were less consistent."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c3",
      "page": 1,
      "why": "Soil biodiversity underpins nutrient cycling, decomposition and crop resilience. Agricultural intensification has been linked to declines in soil invertebrate communities across temperate Europe. Organic systems are widely discussed in the agronomic literature as a possible mitigation, though the evidence base remains uneven and comparisons are complicated by differences in prior land use."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c4",
      "page": 2,
      "why": "Several reviews have examined organic versus conventional systems. These reviews generally report positive but heterogeneous effects, with substantial variation between crops, climates and the duration of organic management."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c5",
      "page": 2,
      "why": "Twelve paired fields were sampled in each of three seasons. Pairs were matched on soil texture, drainage class and elevation. Invertebrate richness was estimated from pitfall trapping and hand sorting of soil cores. Microbial biomass carbon was measured by chloroform fumigation extraction. Earthworm abundance was counted from mustard extraction on a fixed quadrat."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c6",
      "page": 2,
      "why": "Statistical comparisons used paired t-tests on field means. Significance was assessed at alpha equal to 0.05 without correction for multiple comparisons."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c7",
      "page": 3,
      "why": "Species richness rose 31 percent under organic management relative to paired conventional fields, and this difference was statistically significant (p less than 0.01). The effect was consistent across all three seasons."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c8",
      "page": 3,
      "why": "Microbial biomass carbon did not differ significantly between systems in this dataset. The point estimate favoured organic fields but the confidence interval included zero, so we do not interpret this as an effect."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "c9",
      "page": 3,
      "why": "No significant effect of management was observed for earthworm abundance. Counts were highly variable between fields, and the null result should not be read as evidence of equivalence given the limited statistical power."
    }
  ],
